From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 11:45:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Wait for connections to close in a connection
 queue per database

Open and delete requests now follow "open a database connection", "delete a
database" and "close a database connection" in the IndexedDB manager:

- The connection queue of each (origin, name) runs its requests strictly in
  order. Each queued request has a state: queued, waiting for the other
  connections to close, or running its upgrade. Every event that can let the
  front request go on (a versionchange report, a close, a finished
  transaction, a global that went away, the end of an upgrade) ends in
  advance_connection_queue. An open with an upgrade holds the queue until its
  upgrade transaction has finished and its result was sent, so a later
  request's versionchange event reaches the new connection after its success.
- deleteDatabase fires versionchange (newVersion null) at the open connections
  whose close isn't pending, fires blocked once the events were handled if one
  of them is still open, and deletes the database once all have closed.
  Before, it deleted the database under its open connections, which stayed in
  the manager and held back the next open of the name. Its blocked event and
  its result share one callback (DeleteDatabaseEvent).
- A connection is closed once close() was called and all the transactions
  created using it have finished; until then upgrades and deletes wait for it,
  and it gets no versionchange event. Before, the manager forgot it at close(),
  so an upgrade could start while its transactions still ran. A connection
  closed during its upgrade lets the upgrade transaction commit, and the open
  request then fails with AbortError (open step 10.7).
- blocked fires only for connections without a pending close, as Gecko does
  and Chromium does for close() in the versionchange handler: a connection
  that only waits for its transactions closes without the page doing anything.
- versionchange events name the request that fired them, and script's report
  names it back, so a late report of an earlier request's event doesn't count
  for the next one. Script fires no versionchange at a connection whose close
  is pending, but still reports it.
- A Commit for a database that is gone answers DbNotFound, as no transaction
  can outlive its database now.

Integration tests cover the request order, a delete waiting for two
connections (with a report for another request and the database reopened
empty), a closing connection's transactions holding back an upgrade, a close
during the upgrade, and a gone global's connection. The fork tests
connection-queue.any.js and connection-queue-globals.html cover the order of
versionchange and blocked over several connections, interleaved opens and
deletes, a delete, close during an upgrade, and connections in dedicated and
shared workers and a removed iframe.

A connection whose versionchange event can't be sent, as its global's
result channel is gone (which only multiprocess reports), is closed as a
connection of a gone global: its transactions are rolled back and
finished, and it is forgotten, so the request doesn't wait for its
report (P5 review S2). An upgrade whose transaction committed in the
backend before its global went away isn't reverted when the global's
teardown ends the open request; the manager only finishes the upgrade
transaction, which script's teardown doesn't report. The spec keeps a
committed upgrade (step 10.7 only fails the request), and for a new
database the revert deleted the committed database (P5 review N1).

Validation: host nextest -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits 150 passed, 1 skipped (5 new); host SM IndexedDB/ 4,482 of 4,511 (608a255f16b: 4,473; +9 are open-request-queue, close-in-upgradeneeded and idbdatabase_close "Unblock the delete database request", 3 globals each), every result as expected; /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 306 of 306 (+21: connection-queue.any.js 6 subtests x 3 globals, connection-queue-globals.html 3); QuickJS, the OHOS checks, the device unit tests and the board ran at the next commit, which only moves the storage proxy map lookup; after the P5 review fixes: 151 passed, 1 skipped at this commit, with the new test_indexeddb_upgrade_committed_before_its_global_went_away_stays (an upgrade that committed before its global went away keeps its store, and a later readwrite transaction runs; it fails without the fix), and /_mozilla/IndexedDB/removed-iframe-during-upgrade.html, which timed out with a first version of the fix that didn't finish the committed upgrade transaction, passes on the stack tip
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; designed and implemented the connection queue, wrote the integration and fork tests, ran host WPT; the P5 review fixes closed connections of gone globals and kept committed upgrades, with their test)
